<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Celebrations and Traditions – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Celebrations and Traditions</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Celebrate This Tradition Because...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify complex text for peers. Text: Mediate everyday instructions to peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify for peers a complex text about cultural traditions by using visual aids and encouraging questions (e.g., pointing to a traditional dance picture and saying, "This is a celebration dance. What do you notice about the costumes?"). Can mediate for peers instructions related to cultural events by miming actions (e.g., miming dancing steps for "join the dance"). Can simplify cultural descriptions in texts, using gestures and simple language (e.g., showing a photo and saying, "People wear masks here.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the concept of simplifying information for others.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows a picture of a complex traditional Panamanian dance (e.g., Diablicos Sucios dance). Students briefly describe what they see, using vocabulary like "mask," "dance," "traditional," "costume." The teacher then says, "Imagine your younger cousin wants to understand this dance. How would you explain it in very simple words? What gestures would you use?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models simplifying a short, slightly complex sentence about a tradition: "Panamanian folklore is enriched by diverse expressions, including the colorful Pollera, a traditional dress symbolizing national heritage." The teacher then rephrases, pointing to a picture: "This is a Pollera. It's a special, beautiful dress. People wear it for celebrations. It's a symbol of Panama's culture." The teacher emphasizes using gestures and simple language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I simplify, do I use long, complicated words or short, easy words?" (Expected answer: Short, easy words.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone doesn't understand, what can I do besides speaking?" (Expected answer: Use gestures, show pictures, ask questions.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Celebrations and Traditions." The input will be the basis for mediation practice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt (100-120 words) describing "Las Fiestas Patrias" in Panama, including some historical facts and cultural activities. The text will contain present simple (e.g., "Panama celebrates"), past simple (e.g., "Panama became independent"), gerunds (e.g., "about celebrating"), and present perfect (e.g., "People have participated").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage in class discussions about regional and cultural celebrations, responding to questions about traditions, and describing celebrations using varied tenses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text independently.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main topic of this text?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students mark sentences as true or false based on the text (e.g., "Panama celebrates Fiestas Patrias in January." - False).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match key vocabulary words (e.g., "parade," "independence," "symbol") from the text to their simple definitions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation. Students practice simplifying language, mediating instructions, and using visuals and gestures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Simplifying cultural descriptions and mediating instructions using present simple, past simple, gerunds, present perfect, and target vocabulary (tradition, celebration, parade, dance, mask, national, festive, together).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled practice: Sentence Simplification:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 complex sentences from the "Fiestas Patrias" text. In pairs, students rewrite each sentence to simplify it, using basic vocabulary and shorter sentence structures. For example, "The historical significance of November 3rd marks Panama's definitive separation from Colombia, a pivotal moment in its national trajectory" becomes "November 3rd is important because Panama became free from Colombia. This was a big moment for our country." The teacher circulates, checking grammar and vocabulary use (e.g., "became independent").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled practice: Mediating Instructions:</b> Students receive short, slightly complex instructions for a traditional activity (e.g., "To adequately prepare for the parade, participants should first ensure their traditional attire is clean and pressed, next gather their national flags, and finally assemble at the designated meeting point by 7:00 AM."). In new pairs, one student reads an instruction, and the other mediates it verbally using gestures and simple words (e.g., "First, wash your clothes. Next, take your flag. Then, go to the meeting place at 7:00 AM."). They practice word stress on "gather" and "celebration."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair interaction/Peer Check: Cultural Snippet Mediation:</b> Students open their "Cultural Scrapbook" (or a draft of it) to a page with an image and description of a tradition. In pairs, Student A points to an image in their scrapbook and mediates a complex description of a tradition to Student B, using gestures, simple language, and encouraging questions like, "What do you notice about the costumes?" and "Do you understand what 'heritage' means?" Student B listens and then attempts to rephrase what they understood. They switch roles. They practice pronunciation of '-s' endings like /s/ in "decorates" and /z/ in "gathers."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">20 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students use the language in a realistic scenario, focusing on fluency and collaborative interaction related to their "Cultural Scrapbook."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "Cultural Scrapbook Mediation Showcase." Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each student takes turns presenting one page from their "Cultural Scrapbook" to their group. The task is to mediate the information on their page to their peers, imagining their peers are not familiar with the tradition. They must simplify descriptions, explain complex terms, and mediate any implicit instructions (e.g., how to participate in a ritual or dance), using gestures, simple language, and the visual aids in their scrapbook. They should use present simple, past simple, and present perfect to describe what people "celebrate," what "happened," and what they "have participated in."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in mediating information about cultural traditions, using appropriate vocabulary (e.g., "festival," "custom," "ancestry") and grammar (e.g., "I have celebrated this for many years."), clear pronunciation of "cultural" and "celebration," effective use of visuals and gestures, and encouraging peer questions (e.g., "What traditions are important in your community?").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of students' mediation skills and reflection on their learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes groups during the "Cultural Scrapbook Mediation Showcase," noting students' ability to simplify complex information, use visual aids and gestures, and clarify instructions for their peers. A simple checklist or rubric will be used to assess each student's mediation performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (e.g., correct word stress on "mask," "gather," "cultural," and correct /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ sounds for 3rd person singular verbs).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., tradition, celebration, parade, dance, heritage).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of mediation (simplified language, appropriate gestures, visuals).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective explanation of cultural descriptions and instructions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engagement with peers (e.g., asking "What do you notice?").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase you used today to simplify an explanation about your tradition?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using pictures or gestures help you explain your tradition to your classmates?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson's effectiveness and planning for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I use mediation skills outside of the English class to help others understand something new or complex?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most interesting tradition you learned about from a classmate today?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The group mediation activity worked well, allowing students to integrate all previously learned skills. Most students successfully used visuals and gestures. Some struggled with simplifying complex vocabulary directly from their scrapbook descriptions; more explicit modeling on rephrasing advanced terms into basic language might be needed. The cultural scrapbook project was an effective way to integrate all skills from the unit. This lesson successfully concludes the unit on "Celebrations and Traditions."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "I Celebrate This Tradition Because...". The next unit will introduce a new theme and set of communicative objectives, building upon the English language foundation established here.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>